0502                             ; =============================================================================
0502                             ;  Console layer. rawk is the host's key routine: a waiting key in A (zero
0502                             ;  flag clear), or A = 0 with the zero flag set when none is waiting. putc
0502                             ;  (RST 5) prints A. Both keep BC, DE and HL. In the generic build they are
0502                             ;  the host's KEY and CONOUT routines themselves.
0502                             ; 
0502                             ;  A running program reads the keyboard only through getk and pollk, which
0502                             ;  between them give the break key its rules:
0502                             ;    - Ctrl-C stops the program;
0502                             ;    - any other key that the break check reads is held in KEYBUF for the
0502                             ;      next "?", and a newer one takes its place;
0502                             ;    - "?" (getk) takes the held key first;
0502                             ;    - a program that has stored a non-zero byte at BRKOFF gets Ctrl-C as a
0502                             ;      key like any other, but two of them within about 250 ms still stop it.
0502                             ;  Both are called straight from a handler, never from a routine under one:
0502                             ;  the checked build's report counts on finding one return address above the
0502                             ;  data stack, and TOS in HL.
0502                             ; =============================================================================
0502                          IFN   CPM   
                    RAWK:     EQU   KEY   
0502                          ENDIF      
0502                             ; pollk: the break check (see rpushd).
0502   CD 45 05     POLLK:    CALL   rawk   
0505   C8                     RZ      ; no key
0506   32 7A 0A               STA   KEYBUF   ; held for the next "?"
0509   FE 03        PK1:      CPI   3   ; (getk joins here)
050B   C0                     RNZ      
050C                             ; ctlc: Ctrl-C has been read. If the break key is on, that is the end. If
050C                             ; the program has turned it off, wait about 250 ms for one more key: a
050C                             ; second Ctrl-C is the end too; any other key is held (the newest wins),
050C                             ; and the Ctrl-C goes to the program: returns A = 3, zero flag clear.
050C   3A 7B 0A     CTLC:     LDA   BRKOFF   
050F   B7                     ORA   A   
0510   CA 87 05               JZ   brk   
0513   D5                     PUSH   D   
0514   11 10 27               LXI   D,250*MSLOOP   
0517   CD 45 05     CT1:      CALL   rawk   
051A   C2 23 05               JNZ   ct2   
051D   1B                     DCX   D   
051E   7A                     MOV   A,D   
051F   B3                     ORA   E   
0520   C2 17 05               JNZ   ct1   
0523   D1           CT2:      POP   D   ; A = the second key, or 0 if none came
0524   FE 03                  CPI   3   
0526   CA 87 05               JZ   brk   
0529   B7                     ORA   A   
052A   CA 30 05               JZ   ct3   
052D   32 7A 0A               STA   KEYBUF   
0530   3E 03        CT3:      MVI   A,3   
0532   B7                     ORA   A   
0533   C9                     RET      
0534                             ; getk: a key for "?", or A = 0 and the zero flag if there is none.
0534   E5           GETK:     PUSH   H   ; a held key goes first, and is taken out of
0535   21 7A 0A               LXI   H,KEYBUF   ;   the buffer
0538   7E                     MOV   A,M   
0539   36 00                  MVI   M,0   
053B   E1                     POP   H   
053C   B7                     ORA   A   
053D   C0                     RNZ      
053E   CD 45 05               CALL   rawk   
0541   C8                     RZ      
0542   C3 09 05               JMP   pk1   ; Ctrl-C? If not, that is the key
0545                          IF   1-CHECKED   
                    BRK:      EQU   endprog   ; the unchecked build stops without a word;
0545                          ENDIF      
0545                          IF   CHECKED   
0545                BRK:      EQU   e_break   ;   the checked build reports B
0545                          ENDIF      
0545                          IF   CPM   
0545   3E FF        RAWK:     MVI   A,0FFh   ; BDOS 6 with E = FFh: a key, or 0
0547                             ; putc: BDOS 6 for every character. BDOS 2 would check for ^S while
0547                             ; printing and could swallow a key that BDOS 6 then never sees.
0547   C5           PUTC:     PUSH   B   
0548   D5                     PUSH   D   
0549   E5                     PUSH   H   
054A   5F                     MOV   E,A   
054B   0E 06                  MVI   C,6   
054D   CD 05 00               CALL   BDOS   
